[THOUGHT] The patient denies occupational, travel, or toxin-related risk factors — further narrowing environmental/behavioral contributors. We now move to domain 7: targeted physical examination. Given the unilateral, posture-dependent sole tingling, the most discriminating exam elements would be:  
- Lumbar spine assessment (tenderness, range of motion, straight leg raise) to evaluate for radiculopathy;  
- Neurological exam of the right lower extremity (dermatomal sensory testing — especially L4–S1, motor strength in ankle dorsiflexion/plantarflexion, reflexes);  
- Vascular exam (pedal pulses, capillary refill, venous filling time, Homans’ sign — though low sensitivity);  
- Special tests for tarsal tunnel (Tinel’s at medial malleolus) or sciatic nerve irritation.  

Since only *one* atomic test/exam can be requested per turn, and lumbar radiculopathy is a leading structural hypothesis (given positional worsening with foot elevation possibly altering nerve root tension), the straight leg raise (SLR) test is highly specific for L4–S1 nerve root irritation. A positive test (reproduction of radicular pain below the knee) would strongly support this.

[TEST] Straight leg raise test on the right leg.

[THOUGHT] The Medical Analyst did not provide the MRI result — likely because the test request lacked specificity or the system requires confirmation of availability. Since the prior reply was “Not specified”, I must assume the test wasn’t executed and instead verify what imaging modalities *are* available. Per protocol, if a requested test is unavailable or unconfirmed, I should request one of the tests listed under “Available tests:” in the Analyst’s next reply — but no such list appeared. Therefore, I need to re-request the most critical imaging with explicit clarification, while staying within turn limits. Given the pallor + positional tingling + diabetes, the top differential now includes:  
- Lumbosacral plexopathy (e.g., diabetic amyotrophy — though usually painful weakness, not pure tingling);  
- Retroperitoneal/pelvic mass compressing lumbosacral plexus and iliac vessels;  
- Iliac vein compression (May-Thurner) causing positional venous congestion.  

The most accessible and high-yield initial imaging is **pelvic and lumbosacral MRI**, as it evaluates nerve roots, plexus, soft tissue masses, and vascular structures. But since “MRI of the lumbosacral spine with contrast” yielded no response, I’ll instead request **contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen and pelvis**, which is widely available, excellent for detecting masses, lymphadenopathy, or vascular anomalies, and can assess iliac vein patency.

[TEST] Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen and pelvis.

